  <CaseSection number="01" title="GPS does not scale down to a tabletop city." id="problem">
    <p class="case-lead">
      A 1:14-scale traffic environment is a useful place to test planning. It is a terrible place to rely on satellite
      positioning.
    </p>
    <p>
      ADMIT14 is a scaled autonomous driving environment at Esslingen University of Applied Sciences. Vehicles need a
      ground-truth state — position and heading — so motion-planning experiments can be judged against something more
      reliable than the planner's own estimate. Indoor GPS was not an option.
    </p>
    <p>
      The alternative was already mounted on the hall roof: a 4K IP camera looking down at the whole track. If the
      camera could be calibrated precisely enough, every vehicle detected in its image could be projected onto the floor
      plane and tracked over time. One camera, one fixed viewpoint, and a coordinate system shared by the rest of the
      lab.
    </p>
    <div class="case-callout">
      The deliverable was not just a detector. It was a coordinate system the rest of the lab could trust — with a
      measured error small enough to defend the mapping.
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="02" title="One camera, five stages, no hidden state." id="pipeline">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The pipeline runs from an RTSP stream to a filtered vehicle state. Each stage has a measurable failure mode, which
      is why the report treats them separately.
    </p>

    <div class="case-pipeline" aria-label="ADMIT14 pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Acquire</h3>
          <p>Pull frames from a Reolink PoE camera over RTSP using MATLAB's Image Acquisition Toolbox, with a preview stream to check stability.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">4K bird's-eye · RTSP · single camera</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Calibrate</h3>
          <p>Estimate intrinsics from checkerboard views, then extrinsics from the floor plane. Undistort before any pixel is mapped to metres.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">9×6 checkerboard · 300 mm squares · 19 images</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Detect</h3>
          <p>Find the car in the overhead frame. ACF provided the classical baseline; YOLOv4-tiny with transfer learning became the working detector.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">416 px · SGDM · batch 64 · 40 epochs</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Project</h3>
          <p>Map the detected box from image coordinates to the floor plane using the calibrated camera model.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">img2world2d · undistorted frame</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">05</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Track</h3>
          <p>Feed the projected measurements into an Extended Kalman Filter that estimates position, velocity, and heading over time.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">State [x, y, θ] · linear bicycle model</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="03" title="The calibration is the product." id="calibration">
    <p class="case-lead">
      A detector can be approximately right. A coordinate transform cannot. The calibration step received most of the
      engineering attention, and the report's strongest number comes from it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>9×6 checkerboard with 300&nbsp;mm squares</strong> was photographed in <strong>19 positions</strong> across
      the field of view, including the distorted edges. Those views produced the camera intrinsics and lens distortion
      coefficients. A separate floor-plane view produced the extrinsics, which turn an undistorted pixel into a point on
      the track.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mean reprojection error across the 19 calibration images was <strong>0.09&nbsp;pixels</strong>. That is the
      number that makes the rest of the system defensible: the mapping from image to floor is not a hand-tuned
      homography, and the residual is measured rather than assumed.
    </p>

    <div class="case-metrics">
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>0.09 px</strong>
        <span>mean calibration error across 19 views</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>19</strong>
        <span>checkerboard views covering the full field</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>9×6</strong>
        <span>checkerboard · 300 mm squares</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img src="/images/admit14-calibration.webp" alt="3D plot of 19 checkerboard planes positioned around the camera, used to estimate intrinsics and extrinsics" width="1618" height="1271" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The 19 calibration planes in camera coordinates. Coverage at the frame edges is what pulls the distortion
        estimate into shape.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img src="/images/admit14-undistort.webp" alt="Side-by-side overhead frames: the raw camera view with visible barrel distortion at the edges, and the same view after lens correction" width="1718" height="481" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Left: the raw overhead frame, with the track bowing at the edges. Right: the same view after lens correction using
        the calibrated intrinsics and distortion coefficients.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="case-note">
      World-coordinate accuracy was best directly below the camera and degraded toward the periphery — a geometric
      consequence of the viewing angle, not a calibration failure. The report states this openly.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="04" title="A classical baseline, a transfer-learned detector, and an EKF." id="tracking">
    <p class="case-lead">
      Detection was the easy half. Turning a sequence of boxes into a stable heading estimate was the part that needed a
      motion model.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first detector was an <strong>Aggregated Channel Features (ACF)</strong> model: fast, interpretable, and
      CPU-friendly, but limited on a visually variable object and without GPU acceleration. The working
      detector became <strong>YOLOv4-tiny</strong>, fine-tuned with transfer learning at <strong>416&nbsp;px</strong>, using SGDM, a
      learning rate of 1e-4, batch size 64, and 40 epochs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each valid detection is projected to the floor. The Extended Kalman Filter then estimates the
      state <strong>[x, y, θ]</strong>, using a linear bicycle model for the prediction step and the projected box centre as
      the measurement. Heading is part of the state because a car that is moving backwards and a car that is moving
      forwards cast almost the same box.
    </p>

    <div class="case-pair">
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/admit14-labeling.webp" alt="MATLAB Image Labeler showing an overhead track frame with a bounding box drawn around the model car" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Labelling in MATLAB's Image Labeler. One class, one object, and a background that changes with the light.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/admit14-tracking.webp" alt="Bird's-eye track frame with a car bounding box and an overlaid EKF-estimated trajectory" width="1136" height="754" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>The EKF track over the projected measurements. The filter smooths the box jitter; it cannot invent a measurement through an occlusion.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="case-callout">
      The EKF is only as good as its noise model. Covariance tuning was empirical, and poor initialisation could make
      the filter diverge — a failure mode the report documents rather than hides.
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="05" title="What the evidence supports — and what it does not." id="evidence">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The calibration result is quantitative. The detection comparison is largely qualitative. The tracking result is a
      demonstration, not a benchmark. The page keeps those categories separate.
    </p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Supported:</strong> a mean calibration error of 0.09&nbsp;px from 19 views; a working pixel-to-floor mapping; YOLOv4-tiny detecting the car across the evaluated frames; an EKF producing a usable position and heading track.</li>
      <li><strong>Not supported:</strong> a detection mAP, a tracking RMSE, or a latency figure. The report does not publish them, so this page does not invent them.</li>
      <li><strong>Known constraint:</strong> MATLAB's <code>ipcam</code> interface did not expose frame-rate and resolution control. Resolution changes made calibration and detection harder to keep in sync.</li>
      <li><strong>Known constraint:</strong> 4K frames caused jitter and lag in MATLAB, and a single overhead view cannot see through occlusions.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> multi-camera fusion, odometry or LiDAR for a better motion model, a UKF for stronger non-linearities, and a proper throughput measurement on the target machine.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="case-note">
      Detection numbers are reported as the project reported them. No latency, FPS, or accuracy claim has been added.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="06" title="A four-person project, with the seams visible." id="team">
    <p class="case-lead">
      ADMIT14 was built by a team, not a solo repository. Crediting that properly is part of documenting the work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project was completed in the <strong>ASM-VS</strong> programme at Esslingen University of Applied Sciences,
      supervised by <strong>Prof. Dr. Ralf Schuler</strong>. The report authors
      are <strong>M. S. Sri Harish</strong>, <strong>V. Mascarenhas</strong>, <strong>L. Cheng-Yu</strong>, and <strong>K. A. N. Noushiq Mohammed</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      My work covered the camera-to-world mapping, the detector comparison, and the EKF tracking pipeline. The report
      and its evaluation were written jointly by the team.
    </p>
